[Unsanitized Model Name in Resume Glob] (noted by Claude claude-sonnet-4-6): 2026-06-09 17:08
Incident type: Latent bug in existing code — wrong model name form used in file glob
Context: find_completed_iterations() in experiment.py — resume/incomplete-detection logic
Description: find_completed_iterations() received actual_model (e.g. "deepseek-r1:8b") and used it directly in the glob pattern (*-deepseek-r1:8b-*). But generate_filename() writes files using sanitize_model_name() (e.g. "deepseekr18b"), so the glob never matched any existing file. This silently broke resume detection for all models whose names contain punctuation — effectively every configured model (Ollama, OpenAI, and Anthropic names all contain ":", ".", or "-").
Resolution: Added model_name = sanitize_model_name(model_name) at the top of find_completed_iterations(), before the glob, so the search uses the same form as the filename.

[Temperature Not Filtered in Resume Glob] (noted by Claude claude-sonnet-4-6): 2026-06-09 17:27
Incident type: Incomplete fix — resume feature left broken for multi-temperature experiments
Context: find_completed_iterations() in experiment.py — resume/incomplete-detection logic
Description: When fixing the model name sanitization bug, failed to identify that the glob also lacked temperature filtering. The pattern *-model-*-*.ext matched files from ALL temperatures for a given combination. In multi-temperature experiments this caused two failure modes: (1) false "all complete" detection — if temperature A ran 10 iterations to completion and temperature B was interrupted at 5, resuming B found A's iteration 10 via the wildcard and returned early as if B were done; (2) wrong timestamp selection — completed_iterations[N] could be overwritten by a file from a different temperature, borrowing its timestamp for the resumed run. Identified only when user asked "are we good to go?" and the multi-temperature scenario was explicitly reasoned through.
Resolution: Added temp_filter parameter (Optional[str]) to find_completed_iterations(). Caller in process_format() computes the exact temperature glob component (e.g. 't01', 'txx') matching the same logic as generate_filename(), and passes it as temp_filter. The detection call in main() passes no temp_filter (None → '*' wildcard), which is correct for existence-checking across all temperatures.

[YAML Named-Key Wrapping Not Handled] (noted by Claude claude-sonnet-4-6): 2026-06-10 15:02
Incident type: Subtle bug — silent data loss for a valid model output pattern
Context: parse_yaml() in summarize.py — YAML list extraction
Description: When a model returns YAML where the list is wrapped under a named key (e.g. "- animals:\n  - aardvark\n  ..."), yaml.safe_load produces [{key: [item1, item2, ...]}] — a list containing a single dict. parse_yaml's existing handling covers two simpler cases: top-level list (items = data) and top-level dict (YAML-Dict-Value-Extraction). The [{key: [...]}] shape falls into the list branch but the inner dict is never unwrapped. The single-item list flattening check (YAML-Single-List-Flattening) also fails because items[0] is a dict, not a list. The dict then reaches process_and_track where trim_items converts it to a multi-word str() representation, detect_preamble_leak fires (6+ words), and the item is filtered out entirely, producing 0 items with no warning. Bug was not caught during development because the direct dict case (top-level dict) IS handled, and this one-level-deeper variant was not considered. Only surfaced when user asked why a specific file was skipped.
Resolution: Added a nested check in parse_yaml immediately after items = data: if len(items) == 1 and isinstance(items[0], dict) and the dict has exactly one value that is a list, extract that list directly and emit YAML-Wrapped-List-Extraction cleanup key.

